WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
(audience cheers and applauds)

2
00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:03.460
Yeah, hi, Drew, it's so lovely to see you!

3
00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:04.870
Oh, my gosh.

4
00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:09.280
Seth, I feel like I've known you for such a long time.

5
00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:11.890
It's been, we're probably closing in on two decades.

6
00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:14.020
I wouldn't be surprised if it's been 20 years

7
00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:15.150
since we first met.

8
00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:18.413
I think it has been around something like that.

9
00:00:19.374 --> 00:00:24.374
You got 20 Emmy Award nominations for SNL, is that correct?

10
00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:28.862
Thank you for focusing on that and not the one win.

11
00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:30.930
(audience and Drew laughing)

12
00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:33.010
By the way, the Emmy's like are so hard,

13
00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:37.570
I would be with like Amy Poehler and Kristen Wiig and me

14
00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.700
and none of us would ever win

15
00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:42.420
and we'd go back to my house and have a big party.

16
00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:45.710
You know what, a true story, I think the most,

17
00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:47.680
and by the way, it's an honor to be nominated,

18
00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:50.760 line:15%
let me stress that, the most upset I ever was

19
00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:54.030 line:15%
when SNL didn't win an Emmy, I was also at your house.

20
00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:54.863 line:15%
And let me tell you,

21
00:00:54.863 --> 00:00:56.580 line:15%
that was a wonderful constellation prize

22
00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:59.290 line:15%
to get to party at Drew Barrymore's house after a big loss.

23
00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:02.140
That was one of my favorite nights of life,

24
00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:06.700
and Amy Poehler got me this like beautiful decanter

25
00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:09.230
because she kept talking about how

26
00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:11.270
this was such a great antidote.

27
00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:14.530
And then she got me a bottle that said, antidote, on it

28
00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:16.070
and it still sits on my desk.

29
00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:17.670
I just love it so much.

30
00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:20.070
I'm gonna tell you from knowing Amy for years,

31
00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:21.840
if she ever gives you a gift

32
00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:25.169
that's her way of saying, I stole something that night.

33
00:01:25.169 --> 00:01:26.550
(audience laughs)
Good!

34
00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:28.730
Well, I didn't notice anything was missing.

35
00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:32.550
And that decanter with that like engraved antidote

36
00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.410
is like my personal treasure.

37
00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:37.175
If anyone ever stole that, I'd come after them.

38
00:01:37.175 --> 00:01:38.260
(audience laughs)

39
00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:39.320
Just don't have her back over.

40
00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:41.700
No, I want all of you guys.

41
00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:45.750
I mean, you're a part of the legacy and institution.

42
00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:49.300
You know, I did SNL for the first time in '82,

43
00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:52.790
I think it was such a training ground for me.

44
00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:56.830
And you, you know, for two decades, I worked with you

45
00:01:56.830 --> 00:01:58.690
as the head writer

46
00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:03.350
and we did a lot of sketches together also, which was fun.

47
00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:08.010
I remember us playing Posh and Becks in a Donatella sketch.

48
00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:08.850
We both look great,

49
00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:10.550
look how young and great we look.

50
00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:13.100
I know, I haven't worn like a dress like that

51
00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:14.683
probably since that night.

52
00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:18.670
My wife makes me wear that white suit every night.

53
00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:20.100
I'm so happy for you

54
00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.370
because when we knew each other back in the day,

55
00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:24.820
two decades ago, you know, we were both young

56
00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:28.220
and like single and free and like carefree

57
00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:32.750
and, you know, I have two kids, you have a beautiful family

58
00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:35.850
with your gorgeous wife and your two sons

59
00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:38.840
and I was curious because, you know,

60
00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:42.350
we launched the show and I had, you know, my two children,

61
00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:44.840
I was trying to homeschool and launch the show

62
00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:48.010
and it was such a ridiculous juggling act.

63
00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:51.493
And I would see you come out in, is it your in-laws attic?

64
00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:55.140
Yeah, I was in my attic and then my in-laws house

65
00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:57.390
we moved over the course of the summer.

66
00:02:57.390 --> 00:03:01.970
What was it like to do that show with your two kids?

67
00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:04.910
Well first of all, I want to give you a round of applause

68
00:03:04.910 --> 00:03:07.730
for launching a show during a pandemic.

69
00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:09.070
It was hard enough to do a show

70
00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:11.610
but at least we had some understanding

71
00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:13.560 line:15%
about what our show was and how to go about it.

72
00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:14.550 line:15%
So I'm just amazed

73
00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:16.690 line:15%
that you and your entire crew over there pulled it off,

74
00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:18.670 line:15%
that is just such an accomplishment.

75
00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:21.910 line:15%
It was a trip, you know, I think my kids in their head

76
00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:24.480 line:15%
thinks it's pretty cool that their dad has a TV show.

77
00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:27.030 line:15%
But then when I started saying, I'm gonna go up to the attic

78
00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:30.680
to do my TV show I think they thought I was making it up

79
00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:31.530
but they were great.

80
00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:33.090
I mean, they had a real understanding of it.

81
00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:35.840
And the one upside was, you know,

82
00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:38.836
to this awful year is I did see my kids more

83
00:03:38.836 --> 00:03:40.690
than if I was doing it from the studio.

84
00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:42.720
So they appreciated it

85
00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:45.100
and all in all they were pretty good kids about it.

86
00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:46.610
Well, thank goodness for that.

87
00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:48.470
And I just want to ask you a question

88
00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:51.600
as I hold up the Drew's News mug,

89
00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:56.600
why do we crave satirical news, good news, funny news?

90
00:03:56.830 --> 00:04:01.830
What is so powerful about the news in the context of

91
00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:05.713
this sort of information yet pithy delivery?

92
00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.890
The only thing I would say about the way we approach it is

93
00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:14.100
that news more often than not, unfortunately is not good

94
00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:16.750
and we try to run through the news of the day

95
00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:18.350
with as many jokes as possible

96
00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:21.320
so that the watching of it might provide a little catharsis.

97
00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:24.530
It certainly is cathartic for us to write about it that way.

98
00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:27.280
And the fact that there might be an audience

99
00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:29.440
that wants to consume what we're putting together

100
00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:31.274
is incredibly flattering to us.

101
00:04:31.274 --> 00:04:33.857 line:15%
(bright music)

